WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.960
AAAHHHH!!!

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:02.900
Oh my goodness!

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.640
There's something very special about the shot. 
There it is.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:06.600
*in shock* Hold up!

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.160
Clint: They’re not filming in black and white!

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.720
They’re not!

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.660
It’s not in black and white!

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.240
Let's do a slate like this...

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:22.000
Niko: Welcome to VFX artists react to bad and great CGI, where we applaud the genius of good effects...

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.520
and blame the producers for all the bad effects.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:25.800
*Group laughter*

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.840
[Wolverine Origin]

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.620
Niko: Wolverine
Wren: Oh this is Wolverine?!

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.000
Niko: This is a combination of rushed and bad ideas all in one.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.180
Clint: Wh-why are there sparks? 
He barely touched it!

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.840
Clint: You alright in there?
Niko: Flush flush. Everything's fine!

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.120
Niko: These are some straight-up iPhone compositing like, -

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.520
- get an iPhone app to put claws in your hands.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.820
First off, the tracking on his right hand ever so slightly...

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.160
...jiggles.
It's hard to see frame by frame -

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.300
- but in motion it just doesn't quite feel attached.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.700
Even more so there's no freaking shadow on his knuckles!

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.600
Wren: Yeah, yeah, yeah there's no shadow...

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.740
Niko: All you got these just like the CG model sticking out of his hand.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.000
They don't even have proper motion blur highlights in this either.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.640
Like, notice when the motion blur is on the bright parts of the blade,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:15.980
the bright part gets dimmer.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.980
Whereas that bright part should be bright enough cause reflective metal that it stays bright.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.000
Imagine my flashlight is a bright part of a blade -

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.900
- I go like this, it doesn't turn gray.
It stays white.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.920
Wren: I bet they probably didn't have a proper HDRI for the scene.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.660
Basically capturing the proper lighting of the scene -

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:35.780
- But also recreating the materials in a very scientifically accurate way.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.200
They probably just kind of winged it.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.640
Maybe they were running out of time, they had to do the scene really quickly.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.240
Niko: The biggest question is why aren't they practical?
Wren: That's exactly it!

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.080
They have a special type of blade holder -

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.720
- that's like you literally just hold your fist and the blades like can form perfectly around your knuckle and it looks real!

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.220
Clint: That's a great point do it practical when you can.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.020
Dude this scene...

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:03.660
...sweet.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.920
Wraah, oh, are you serious? No!

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.300
NOOOOO!

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.500
Niko: The guy is watching all this happen, he's so calm.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.240
Clint: Oh what it floated for a second!

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.220
Wren: Yeah it was kind of...(battle sounds)

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.780
Clint: They animated position and scale,

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:22.740
you know, it's just like a Stock image of a helicopter that they they made grow larger.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.460
Wren: Do you recognize those mountains in the background at all?

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:29.180
They filmed lots of scenes here including scenes from like Chronicles of Narnia -

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.880
- famously Lord of the Rings, they filmed a lot of stuff in that area and that specific Valley is where Isengard is located.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:38.100
Oh, yes speaking of which Lord of the Rings, Hobbit. We've seen your comments.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.980
It's been very highly commented about.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.800
So we're definitely gonna be doing an episode comparing the two very shortly.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.880
And if you'd like to see other movies TV shows,

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.880
anything at all for us to react to please leave a comment below we read them all.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.080
What's wrong with his eyes?

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.900
Not his eyes,

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.320
Sir Patrick Stewart eyes.
Niko: Just Mr. Clean.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.580
I think it's just an example of like,

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.060
they're probably just going in and taking out all the wrinkles and doing some patchwork on his face,

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.080
but in doing so they're not staying quite entirely true to the lighting on his face -

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.520
- and where the shadows would be and where the wrinkles would be.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.080
Niko: This shot is soooo bad!

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:18.560
[Group laughter and cringe]

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.940
There’s no shadows!

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.500
There's fo- there's footprints!

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.420
They're leaving footprints when they run.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.200
Why are they leaving footprints and not even having a shadow?

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.000
Like at least a fuzzy soft dark matte underneath their feet would help.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.870
This movie had the same budget as transformers and this is the quality that the effects in this movie.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.500
This is the like the last stage of an artist just eyeballing it and being like,

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.520
'I guess the background and probably be this bright, let's bump it up!'

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:51.200
Versus like, 'Hey, I went out there and got like light meter readings, and I took my photo samples and I use my HDRI, -

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.400
- and I have a scientifically accurate and measured moment for what that should look like.'

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.090
Transformers looks so good -

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.429
- because that's what they did for all the Transformers.

00:03:59.429 --> 00:04:04.200
Everything was physically accurate measured with HDRI's, proper surface simulations and -

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.500
- mmh that looks good!

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.400
Wren: Well, that's a real truck.
Clint: That's a real truck.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.400
Niko: Michael Bay does what I think is the perfect approach for CG which is -

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.200
- blending practical and visual effects together in one shot.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.140
Like that truck is real... just kidding, the truck is NOT real!

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.000
[Group Laughs] 
Wren &amp; Clint: Got 'em!

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.400
Niko: This bus shot, something special about this bus shot, -

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.800
That bus is real! That explosion is real!

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.500
That bus, they already had it chopped in half and they tear it apart in the middle of the scene. That's all real!

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.600
The only thing fake is the robot going through it.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:42.000
Clint: Oh, wow, they have wires, they have wires on the edges of the bus pulling it apart -

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.600
- it looks so good.
Wren: And this movie is 12 years old by the way.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.200
Clint: Something that's helping Michael Bay, he's putting the camera in a realistic place.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:54.000
It's very grounded. You're in a car, you're in a chase cam, you know, you're seeing it from these angles.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.000
That's so cool!

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.600
N: And the huge challenge with that though is you're literally filming something that's not there.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.900
So you have to have, what we call our "CG goggles",

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.000
You have your CG goggles on and you have to be imagining that robot coming through.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.600
When Optimus gets tackled there, right here -

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.600
- it's like slightly out of frame. It feels flawed in a real way.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.100
W: I don't think people give Michael Bay enough credit for his filmmaking skills.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.000
C: Absolutely not.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:22.300
[Attack Bear Noises]

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:30.000
C: Oh my god, this is brutal man.
N: And that fur looks so good.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.800
W: That wet matted fur.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.800
N: Dude, right here, where it like kind of picks him up and like bites and moves him. That looks really good.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.100
He's like, 'Get in the frame! I need to see your reaction!'

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.300
[Group Laughs]

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.900
W: Also, it hasn't cut yet by the way.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.800
Oh, dude! The fogging of the lens. Wow!

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.600
N: Do you think that was real fog? No, it can't because the bear wasn't real, duh.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:53.520
C: What an effective... [gagging noise]

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.600
C: What an effec- [gagging noise]

00:05:57.169 --> 00:05:59.200
What an effective scene though, man.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:04.570
W: What sells this shot is the fact that Leo is actually being moved around physically in real life.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.800
C: Hey, wasn't there a guy in place of the bear?
W: I mean Arthur worked on this movie.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.800
Call Arthur! Call Arthur! Right now, do it.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.300
N: I'll do it. I'll call Arthur real quick.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.000
Arthur VO: Hi, how's it going?
N: It's good. It's good.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.100
Hey, so we're here watching the bear scene from the REVENANT and we're wondering, -

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:25.300
- did they have like a dude that was pulling Leonardo DiCaprio around or was like he on wires? Like how did they do it?

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:31.900
Arthur VO: So he had a guy in like a blue suit with bear arms, that was kind of one part of it -

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:34.500
- just kind of being the inspiration for the movements.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:35.700
N: He had bear arms?

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:36.200
Arthur: Yeah.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.200
[Group Amazed]

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:42.700
Arthur: Leo was in a harness that was pretty complicated -

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:47.400
- wire contraption to several different trees so that the wires would pull him in different directions.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.500
One of the things that was really critical was rehearsal because we had to know what the, quote on quote, -

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:59.200
- fight choreography was between the bear and Leo so that the guy in the suit and Leo being pulled can all be in sync, -

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.400
so that, when they did put in a bear it would line up.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:03.900
N: So are there some hidden cuts on this like long take?

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.600
Arthur: Tons of hidden cuts. Tons of blends and merges.

00:07:07.669 --> 00:07:10.210
N: Thanks for the info man, trying to figure out how they did it.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:11.400
Arthur: Yeah, of course.

00:07:11.419 --> 00:07:14.800
N: A match cut is basically when you have a cut from one piece of footage to another -

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.460
- but an element in the footage is basically in the same spot.

00:07:18.469 --> 00:07:21.000
C: Like in 2001,  the bone turns into a spaceship

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.900
N: But match cuts can also be a visual effects technique or basically a magic trick where, ya know -

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.500
- you're doing basically like a whip pan, and right in the middle of that whip -

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.700
- and you're just match cutting right in the middle that whip when things look the same.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.400
Like that's still a match cut. It's not like an artsy match cute..

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.100
C: It's a hidden match cut.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:38.600
N: But sometimes hidden cuts are hidden by making the match cuts, -

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:40.700
- sometimes they're hidden by things wiping the frame,

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:45.100
And I think it's time for us to look at the master class of match cuts and hidden cuts.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:49.900
C: Kingsman is a great movie. Super stylized Matthew Vaughn tearing it up!

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:54.250
W: Oh man, look at that high shutter speed style. Everything's just super jittery.

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:56.250
[Niko chuckling]

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:59.949
N: Yeah, the CG doesn't look so great there but you know what, who cares!

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:04.150
He's gonna dislocate a shoulder. Oh ma-

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:08.160
[Group in shock]

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:09.320
C: That's Incredible.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.700
W: How is that the most visceral moment of this entire scene?

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:16.100
People are getting their heads blown up, and that woman just got her neck chopped, -

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:18.480
- but somehow a dude crumpling up like that,

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:19.840
Against a wall!

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.100
N: Dummy shots are so funny.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:25.500
W: It's a realistic-looking head dummy.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.319
All right, I think we should go back to beginning this scene,

00:08:27.319 --> 00:08:29.600
just take a moment and show you where abunch of the hidden cuts are,

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:30.900
and then talk about how they did them.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:32.460
All right, right there.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:34.000
Right there for sure.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:35.600
Right there, cut.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:37.800
Boom, cut.

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:40.840
W: I think the reason why there are so many hidden cuts in this scene is because, -

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:43.640
- this is an incredibly complex choreography.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:47.810
C: It's like in a video game. You can't beat the whole level without save points.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:52.840
W: I bet a lot of these different stunts require very specific setups that you can't do with -

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:54.980
- the camera going through the entire scene.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:57.680
N: And what they're doing here, like, our eyes are super sensitive to motion, -

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.620
- to the point where we don't really read details in them.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:04.440
So that guy wiping the frame, as he wipes the frame, they're rotoscoping the edge of him -

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.780
So everything gets revealed as he wipes is from the next shot.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:12.060
It's one thing to have hidden cuts, it's another thing to have the intricacy of this puzzle of a fight scene,

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:15.860
where you're doing all these tricks and taking advantage of those hidden cuts.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:20.040
W: Wait a minute. Wait a minute. Is that Lieutenant Dan?

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:23.320
N: Forrest Gump, it's actually a treasure trove of amazing visual effects shots.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:25.400
There's something very special about this shot.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:26.240
There it is.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:29.140
W: *in shock* Hold up, hold up!

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:30.920
N: The actor has legs by the way.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:37.760
